{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|''^##|''^###|Writer|direct|existing|proportional|one-file'\"' '/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md' '/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md'\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1:# Reference frame bootstrap\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:20:to resolve from this directory to the same target. Fenced code, inline\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:55:### Canonical statement\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:100:### Product lineage objects\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:154:## 2. Required definition relations\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:199:## 9. Recursive discovery and explicit composition\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:217:- `relation`: the authority defining the directed composition kind, the\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:268:## 11. One constitutional surface and version boundary\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:287:access; otherwise the required meaning is supplied directly (P4b, P1;\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:329:### Authority kinds\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:342:its existing owner, and the frame does not create that grant (P6, P3).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:374:Proxy limit. Human authority may act directly, or through an explicitly\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:377:(P6, P3). A proxy acts only inside its existing grant. It cannot change\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:382:direct human acceptance where the governing product or release law\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:396:### The frame tuple\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:421:## 11. The activation packet\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:448:### Minimal activation binding\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:456:# Reference-Frame Activation: <stable activation identity>\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:458:## Frame\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:462:## Subject And Basis\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:466:## Attention Surface\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:471:## Actor And Authority\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:477:## Evidence And Relations\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:482:## Stop And Change\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:495:### Intake\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:536:### Admission\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:576:## 2. Context construction under an envelope\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:657:   ([RF-017](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-017-proportional-compression));\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:697:directly: the owning sections that decide the task's questions, which are\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:785:### Staleness of derived surfaces\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:800:  until then the actor acquires the deciding owner sections directly\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:824:### Results\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:827:directly or as a refined value mapped into it (P5):\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:991:### Foundation to law: binding a project to the method\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1027:### Foundation to law\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1061:| P6 (independent), P3 (independent); instance premise c | By P6 authority exists only through an admitted grant, and by P3 it binds its owner and scope, so a frame carries and applies admitted authority and never mints, widens, transfers or reconstructs it from actor identity, capability, evidence shape, implementation reachability or frame selection; declaring, selecting, activating or executing a frame, or naming a role, grants none. The kinds of authority may coincide in a lawful case and never collapse by default, and actor identity is not an authority. A read-only evaluation declares no operation authority; an effectful one inherits the exact operation grant from its existing owner. A proxy for human authority keeps its identity distinct from the human authority's | [RF-006](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-006-authority-conservation); [FOUNDATIONS §2 Authority kinds](../FOUNDATIONS.md#authority-kinds) and [Frame objects](../FOUNDATIONS.md#frame-objects), Role, persona, abstraction, component, outside this member; [§2](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#2-core-claims) claim 8; [§12](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#12-violations) item 6 | the division into frame-set, semantic, evaluation, operation and decision authority, with actor identity apart, and frame-set authority as human authority: FOUNDATIONS §2 Authority kinds; when a bounded proxy may act: [IDENTITY §6](../IDENTITY.md#6-authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004) | 7, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1062:| P6 (independent) | By P6 meaning, evidence, application, operation and decision are separate authorities, so semantic law defines what the evaluation means, evidence supplies observations, the evaluator applies the declared relation, the existing operation owner supplies any operation grant, and decision authority disposes the result; a frame declaration keeps these roles distinguishable even when one actor performs several. The evaluator applies the frame's declared invariants, evidence rules and result algebra and records every assumption whose failure could change the result | [RF-007](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-007-semantic-evidence-and-verdict-separation); [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) Evaluation, outside this member | none; [EVALUATION §2](../EVALUATION.md#2-separation) applies this law to review (family F6) | 7, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1064:| P0 (recovered), P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise), P6 (independent); instance premise a | By P0 an actor's envelope is finite, and by P5 a step cannot report success on what it did not hold, so frame adequacy includes actor usability. When the material manifold exceeds an actor's envelope, the lawful responses are another actor, a refined frame or outer contract, sequenced activations, a lawful compression or `out_of_frame`; silently omitting material relations to fit is never one of them, and a frame never silently grows beyond the selected actor's envelope. Actor capability may justify a different frame size or activation sequence, and can neither waive an invariant nor widen authority | [RF-014](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-014-capability-fit); [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) step 3, the responses when the context does not fit, outside this member; [FOUNDATIONS §2 Frame objects](../FOUNDATIONS.md#frame-objects), Capability envelope, outside this member; [§11](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#11-the-activation-packet), frame size; [§12](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#12-violations) item 10 | the selection among these responses: human authority, directly or in advance through the grant; which responses a step selects within its grant (refinement, sequencing, compression) and which re-enter the owner of the grant or envelope (another actor, a larger envelope): ACTIVATION §2 step 3 | 7, 8 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1065:| P2 (independent), P0 (recovered), P6 (independent) | By P2 a compression serves the evaluation, and by P0 it must fit the envelope, so a compression preserves the material distinctions and failure detections the evaluation family requires while fitting the envelope; it need not be a unique smallest frame. Intentional overlap is kept where it supplies independently activated evaluation, different capability fit, different evidence altitude or useful fault detection, and frames are merged only when the merged frame preserves evaluation meaning, authority, capability fit and proportional reasoning cost. Whether a compression preserved what the evaluation needs is a claim: the compressing step records the compression and its preservation argument | [RF-017](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-017-proportional-compression); [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) step 3, compression, outside this member; [§12](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#12-violations) item 13 | whether that claim is assessed independently: the promotion it serves ([EVALUATION §3](../EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment)) | 6, 7 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1066:| P0 (recovered, with its actor-change premise), P3 (independent), P6 (independent); instance premise e | By P0's actor-change premise and P3 an input is acquired by exact identity from the selected basis, and by P6 absence from view grants nothing, so declared inputs are acquired by exact identity, and only from the exact selected basis: a declared input absent from the actor's view is neither licence to read elsewhere nor evidence that it is absent, and a candidate source, a moving selector or another basis is no substitute. Where a derived surface is stale, missing or insufficient for a named question, the deciding owner sections are acquired directly. Acquisition is read-only, grants no role, operation or decision, and a missing binding is returned with its outcome before any dependent action | [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) step 4 and Acquisition, outside this member | acquiring a current derived surface in preference to its owning sections where it serves the task: ACTIVATION §2 Acquisition; which derived surfaces are published: their publisher, and none owns a determination | 2, 7 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:108